+     * QWERTZ: the German Latin layout — QWERTY with Y and Z swapped, plus
+     * ü ö ä ß on keys of their own. The umlaut twins (`wär`/`war`,
+     * `schön`/`schon`) are 7.1 % of German tokens and share a key centre
+     * with their base letter when they ride a long press, which no shape can
+     * tell apart. Dedicated keys separate them: on 1,324 synthetic strokes
+     * the German profile reads 96.37 % on the long-press grid and 97.73 %
+     * here (top-4 is 100 % either way, the strokes being synthetic). The
+     * grid is 11 keys wide, as desktop QWERTZ is: ü after p, ö and ä after
+     * l, ß at the end of the bottom row. Rows are ~9 % narrower than the old
+     * ten-key grid.
+     *
+     * The umlauts must not stay in the long-press lists: `GlideKeyMap.of`
+     * maps a character to the first key that types it, so a leftover `ü` on
+     * `u` would keep the decoder reading the base key and leave the new key
+     * invisible.
      */
